<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Calagurris</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Calagurris Nassica</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Calagurris was a town of the Vascones on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, the site of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calahorra">Calahorra</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-rioja">La Rioja</a>. It backed Sertorius in his war against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-senate">Roman Senate</a> and fell to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a>'s forces in 72 BCE after a siege so harsh that the defenders were said to have eaten their dead. As a Roman municipium it was the birthplace of the rhetorician Quintilian.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/calagurris.jpg" alt="Calagurris" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zarateman · CC BY-SA 3.0 es · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Calahorra_-_Catedral_10.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
